# Forth Reference Documentation

This document provides comprehensive reference documentation for all Forth words supported by EIGHTBOL, covering each word variant with formal stack effect notation, operand type handling, numeric conversion rules, error conditions, performance characteristics, and optimization opportunities.

## Document Structure

Each word entry includes:
- Formal stack effect notation
- All usage variations
- All operand types
- Numeric type conversion rules (BINARY unsigned/signed, DECIMAL BCD unsigned/signed, DISPLAY)
- Precision and scale handling
- Overflow/underflow behavior
- Sign handling
- BCD-specific considerations
- DISPLAY format considerations
- Multiple examples with different numeric types
- Error conditions and messages
- Performance characteristics
- Optimization opportunities

## Numeric Type System

EIGHTBOL supports three numeric representations in Forth:

### BINARY Unsigned
- Range: 0 to 65535 (16-bit)
- No sign bit
- Standard binary representation
- All bitwise operations work on unsigned values

### BINARY Signed
- Range: -32768 to 32767 (16-bit two's complement)
- MSB indicates sign (1 = negative, 0 = positive)
- Arithmetic operations respect sign
- Comparison operations differ from unsigned

### DECIMAL BCD Unsigned
- Range: 0 to 9999
- Each nibble represents one decimal digit
- No sign bit
- Limited range but exact decimal representation
- Used for financial and display calculations

### DECIMAL BCD Signed
- Range: -9999 to 9999
- Most significant nibble of high byte indicates sign (D = negative)
- Complements used for negative values
- Range is half of unsigned BCD

### DISPLAY Format
- Values displayed in current radix
- Default is DECIMAL
- Can be changed with BASE word
- Display width depends on value magnitude
- Leading zeros suppressed unless padded

